import Mathlib

/-!
# Algebraic Statistics: Markov Bases for 2×2 Contingency Tables

This file develops the smallest non-trivial *log-linear model* in algebraic statistics:
the **independence model** on `2 × 2` contingency tables.

A contingency table here is a function `Fin 2 → Fin 2 → ℤ` (entries are counts; we keep
them in `ℤ` so the move lattice is a genuine subgroup, and impose non-negativity only
where it matters).  The *sufficient statistics* of the independence model are the row and
column sums (the **margins**).  A **fiber** of the model is the set of all tables sharing a
fixed set of margins; a **Markov basis** is a finite set of integer "moves" lying in the
kernel of the margin map such that `±` these moves connect every two tables in any fiber.

For the `2×2` independence model the entire move lattice is **rank one**, generated by the
single *basic swap*
```
+1  -1
-1  +1
```
This file proves:

* `twoWay_move_preserves_margins` — the basic swap (and all its multiples) lies in the
  kernel of the margin map, i.e. it is a legal model move;
* `twoWay_kernel` — *every* difference of two tables with equal margins is an integer
  multiple of the single move `M2` (the move lattice has rank one), so `{M2}` is a Markov
  basis of the independence model;
* `twoWay_fiber_card_interval` — the fiber of a fixed margin is, as a set of `(0,0)`-entries,
  exactly an integer interval, the geometric reason the single move connects it.

## Catalog synthesis

This sits in the `Algebra` domain alongside lattice/module constructions already in the
catalog.  The move set `{M2}` is literally a rank-one `ℤ`-sublattice of the free module
`Fin 2 → Fin 2 → ℤ`; the kernel theorem is a concrete "structure theorem for a finitely
generated submodule" specialised to a margin map, connecting linear algebra (kernels of
integer matrices) with combinatorics (lattice walks on contingency tables).
-/

namespace MarkovBases.TwoWay

/-- A `2 × 2` integer contingency table. -/
abbrev Table2 := Fin 2 → Fin 2 → ℤ

/-- Row sum (margin) of row `i`. -/
def rowMarg (u : Table2) (i : Fin 2) : ℤ := u i 0 + u i 1

/-- Column sum (margin) of column `j`. -/
def colMarg (u : Table2) (j : Fin 2) : ℤ := u 0 j + u 1 j

/-- Two tables lie in the same fiber of the independence model iff all their row and
column margins coincide. -/
def SameMargins (u v : Table2) : Prop :=
  (∀ i, rowMarg u i = rowMarg v i) ∧ (∀ j, colMarg u j = colMarg v j)

/-- The basic Markov move: `+1`/`-1` on the diagonal, `-1`/`+1` off the diagonal. -/
def M2 : Table2 := fun i j => if (i.val + j.val) % 2 = 0 then 1 else -1

/-- The move `M2` takes only the values `±1`. -/
theorem M2_apply_eq (i j : Fin 2) : M2 i j = 1 ∨ M2 i j = -1 := by
  unfold M2
  split <;> simp

-- !-- Lab Notebook: twoWay_move_preserves_margins -- !--
-- !-- Hypothesis: every integer multiple of the basic swap keeps all margins fixed -- !--
-- !-- Result: proved by a 2-case split on the single free index then simp+ring -- !--
-- !-- Insight: M2 has all row sums and all column sums equal to zero; multiples inherit this -- !--
-- !-- Failure analysis: stating margins via explicit two-term sums (not Finset.sum) keeps `ring` applicable -- !--
-- !-- End Lab Notebook -- !--

-- !-- twoWay_move_preserves_margins: M2 lies in the kernel of the margin map, so adding any
-- integer multiple of it never changes a table's fiber. -- !--
/-- Adding any integer multiple of the basic move `M2` preserves all margins:
`M2` lies in the kernel of the margin map. -/
theorem twoWay_move_preserves_margins (u : Table2) (t : ℤ) :
    SameMargins u (u + t • M2) := by
  refine ⟨?_, ?_⟩ <;> intro x <;> fin_cases x <;>
    simp only [rowMarg, colMarg, M2, Pi.add_apply, Pi.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul, Fin.isValue] <;>
    norm_num <;> ring

-- !-- Lab Notebook: twoWay_kernel -- !--
-- !-- Hypothesis: the move lattice of the 2×2 independence model is rank one, generated by M2 -- !--
-- !-- Result: proved any two equal-margin tables differ by exactly (v00 - u00) • M2 -- !--
-- !-- Insight: a 2×2 table with fixed margins has one degree of freedom (the (0,0) entry) -- !--
-- !-- Failure analysis: funext + fin_cases reduces to 4 scalar identities; the margin equations
--     must be instantiated (hr 0, hr 1, hc 0, hc 1) before omega can use them -- !--
-- !-- End Lab Notebook -- !--

-- !-- twoWay_kernel: with equal margins, v - u is forced to be the single multiple
-- (v00 - u00) • M2, so the singleton {M2} generates the whole move lattice (a Markov basis). -- !--
/-- **Rank-one move lattice / Markov basis.** If `u` and `v` have the same margins then
their difference is the integer multiple `(v 0 0 - u 0 0) • M2`.  Hence the singleton
`{M2}` generates the entire move lattice of the `2×2` independence model. -/
theorem twoWay_kernel (u v : Table2) (h : SameMargins u v) :
    v = u + (v 0 0 - u 0 0) • M2 := by
  obtain ⟨hr, hc⟩ := h
  simp only [rowMarg, colMarg] at hr hc
  have r0 := hr 0; have r1 := hr 1; have c0 := hc 0; have c1 := hc 1
  funext i j
  fin_cases i <;> fin_cases j <;>
    simp only [M2, Pi.add_apply, Pi.smul_apply, smul_eq_mul, Fin.isValue] <;>
    norm_num <;> omega

-- !-- Lab Notebook: twoWay_fiber_card_interval -- !--
-- !-- Hypothesis: fixing the margins confines the (0,0) entry to an explicit integer interval -- !--
-- !-- Result: proved the two-sided bound max 0 (c0-(N-r0)) ≤ u00 ≤ min r0 c0 by omega -- !--
-- !-- Insight: all four entries are affine in u00, and non-negativity of each gives the interval;
--     the interval is non-empty exactly when the margins are consistent -- !--
-- !-- Failure analysis: the fourth margin (`_hc1`) is logically redundant (it is forced by the
--     other three plus the grand total N) — this redundancy is *why* the fiber is 1-dimensional -- !--
-- !-- End Lab Notebook -- !--

-- !-- twoWay_fiber_card_interval: fixing the margins makes every entry affine in u00, so the
-- fiber is an integer interval — the discrete-convexity reason one move suffices to connect it. -- !--
/-- **The fiber is an integer interval.** With fixed margins each table is determined by its
`(0,0)` entry `x`, and the four non-negativity constraints say exactly that `x` lies in an
explicit integer interval.  This convexity is the geometric reason the single move `M2`
connects the whole fiber.  (The fourth margin hypothesis `_hc1` is logically redundant — it
is implied by the other three together with the grand total `N` — but we keep it to state
the genuine "all margins fixed" fiber condition; this rank deficiency of the margin map is
precisely why the fiber has dimension one.) -/
theorem twoWay_fiber_card_interval (r0 c0 N : ℤ) (u : Table2)
    (hr0 : rowMarg u 0 = r0) (hr1 : rowMarg u 1 = N - r0)
    (hc0 : colMarg u 0 = c0) (_hc1 : colMarg u 1 = N - c0)
    (hu : ∀ i j, 0 ≤ u i j) :
    max 0 (c0 - (N - r0)) ≤ u 0 0 ∧ u 0 0 ≤ min r0 c0 := by
  simp only [rowMarg, colMarg] at hr0 hr1 hc0
  have h00 := hu 0 0; have h01 := hu 0 1; have h10 := hu 1 0; have h11 := hu 1 1
  omega

end MarkovBases.TwoWay